From: PW3 Android port
Subject: [PW3] Launcher3: the home screen has a way into all applications

Everything installed after this image was built is in the list of all
applications, and nothing on the device says the list is there ("现在主要问题
是，用这个系统的用户不知道如何唤出全部应用", user, 2026-10-22). A swipe up
opens it (0006) and no mark hints at the swipe: the caret AOSP floated above
the hotseat belonged to the pull that 0006 took out, and the user asked for no
hint in its place (Test V39).

So the home screen gets an entry of its own: a cell like the four applications
above it, labelled 全部应用, drawn as nine squares -- the mark this list has
had on every Android home screen.

What it costs:

  * nothing the four entries already in the layout do not. The entry is a row
    in the workspace database (<shortcut>: a title, an icon resource and an
    intent), bound by the same loader and drawn by the same BubbleTextView as
    an application's icon;
  * a tap starts nothing at all. The intent names no activity; it carries one
    action, Utilities.ACTION_ALL_APPS_ENTRY, which the launcher recognises in
    startAppShortcutOrInfoActivity and answers with onClickAllAppsButton --
    the call AOSP's own all-apps button made -- so the list opens exactly as a
    swipe opens it, in one panel update.

Three things cannot take the entry off the screen, for one reason: it is the
only thing on the screen that says the list exists, so losing it leaves the
device with no visible way in. A long press does not pick it up (so no drag can
drop it on 移除), accessibility does not offer to remove it, and dropping
another icon on it does not make a folder of the two -- which would put it
inside the folder and leave the home screen as bare as before. AOSP's own
all-apps button could not be dragged off either, while it still had a cell.

The entry can still be moved out of the way by what moves the rest of the home
screen: a drop that needs its cell pushes it along with the others, inside the
page.

The hotseat was the other candidate for the cell and is the wrong one: a
hotseat cell draws no label (CellLayout, setTextVisibility(mContainerType !=
HOTSEAT)), and the one thing this entry has to do is say what it is.

The label follows the language in use. A <shortcut> keeps its title in the
database as the string the row was written with, so an entry seeded in Chinese
would read Chinese in an English system; the loader reads this one's title from
the resource instead (LoaderTask).

How the entry reaches a device that already has a home screen is 0015's
question and not this one's. Up to here that was done by moving SCHEMA_VERSION,
which seeds the workspace again from an empty table and costs the reader
everything they had arranged and installed; 0015 gives the layout a version of
its own and delivers what a new version added, so this entry arrives without
the home screen being taken apart to carry it.

